==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|fro|en}} {{m|fro|lapidaire}}, from {{etyl|la|en}} {{m|la|lapidārius||of stones}} (later used as a noun ‘stone-cutter’), from {{m|la|lapis||stone}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ˈlæpɪdəɹi/|lang=en}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|lapidaries}}

# A person who [[cut]]s, [[polish]]es, [[engrave]]s, or [[deal]]s in [[gem]]s.
#* '''2013''', Peter G. Read,''Gemmology'', Elsevier, p.289
#*: In the very early days of gemstone fashioning, a polisher or '''lapidary''' would cut and polish both diamonds and other gemstones.
# An [[expert]] in gems or precious stones; a [[connoisseur]] of lapidary work.
# {{lb|en|archaic}} A [[treatise]] on precious stones.

